            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Sunzi's Life</strong><br>\nSun Tzu was a Chinese general, military strategist, and philosopher who lived in the Eastern Zhou period of ancient China. Most people believe that he was born around 544 BCE and died around 496 BCE. Not much is known about his life, and some historians even doubt whether he was a real historical figure. The details that exist come from historical texts like the Records of the Grand Historian and The Spring and Autumn Annals.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"The Art of War\"</strong><br>\nThe Art of War is an ancient Chinese military treatise attributed to Sun Tzu. Composed of 13 chapters, each one is devoted to an aspect of warfare and how it applies to military strategy and tactics. Written in the 5th century BCE, the book has been praised for its strategic depth and understanding of warfare, and it remains influential in both warfare and beyond to this day.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Principles of Warfare</strong><br>\nSun Tzu's work emphasizes the importance of positioning in strategy and argues that the skillful strategist should be able to subdue the enemy's army without any battle. His teachings prioritize winning without conflict when possible, suggesting that the acme of skill is to defeat the enemy without a fight, by exploiting strategic advantages and understanding the enemy's plans.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
