          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Tactical Deception</strong><br>\nOne of Sun Tzu's key strategies involves using deceit: He advocated for the use of spies and other forms of intelligence gathering to mislead opponents. According to Sun Tzu, all warfare is based on deception, hence the importance of adapting to one's situation and keeping strategies flexible.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Strategic Attack</strong><br>\nSun Tzu's ideas about strategic attack focus on the importance of knowing when to engage the enemy and when to avoid battle. He suggests that the best policy is to attack the enemy's strategy itself, to disturb their plans and organization well before the physical battle ensues.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Preparation and Planning</strong><br>\nAccording to Sun Tzu, preparation and planning are key to success in war. He suggested that war should be avoided unless it is absolutely necessary and that thorough preparation and calculated strategy could lead to victory with minimal loss of life.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
