          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Competitive Advantage</strong><br>\nIn \"The Art of War,\" competitive advantage comes from understanding and leveraging one's own strengths while exploiting the weaknesses of the enemy. Sun Tzu advised leaders to be as flexible as water, adapting to the terrain and the enemy's condition to maintain the upper hand.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use of Spies</strong><br>\nSun Tzu greatly valued the role of espionage in war. He believed that what enabled the wise sovereign and the good general to strike and conquer and achieve things beyond the reach of ordinary men was foreknowledge, which could not be elicited from ghosts and spirits, but from people knowledgeable about the enemy's situation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact and Legacy</strong><br>\nSun Tzu's teachings have transcended military theory and have been adopted in various fields such as business, law, and sports coaching. His ideas on strategy, competition, and leadership continue to inspire many in different areas of life and have cemented his legacy as a profound thinker and strategist.</p>\n"
              },
